{"kind":"assert","text":"A bare retirement I created and closed within the minute, recorded because the retirement text names a successor that was not attached to it.\n\nWHAT HAPPENED. I superseded ef88847c at internal/app, intending to name its corrected successor in rests_on. The successor had not been created: the publish before it was refused for a stale basis, and I passed the refusal message into the supersede as though it were an event id. So the supersede landed with no successor attached — a bare retirement — and internal/app had no live artifact at head b2cee375 for about a minute. The successor now stands at 1fd4988f and the path is covered.\n\nWHY IT HAPPENED, precisely. I chained two commands and used the output of the first as input to the second without checking that the first had succeeded. The refusal was printed, I did not read it, and a string beginning \"gs: this state rests on...\" went where an event id belonged. The write boundary had told me exactly what was wrong and I fed its complaint forward as data.\n\nWHAT I SHOULD HAVE DONE. The rule I have been applying to other people's claims all night applies here: check the thing before building on it. A command's output is not a result until you have looked at whether it is one. Every id I pass to a supersede should be one I have seen resolve, not one I have assumed exists because the command that would have produced it ran.\n\nTHE RETIREMENT TEXT IS NOW WRONG IN THE LOG. It says \"superseded by the corrected internal/app artifact named here\" and names nothing. That sentence cannot be corrected — the act is immutable — so this assert is the correction: the successor it meant is 1fd4988f, at the same head, resting on the same promise.\n\nNO PATH WAS LEFT UNCOVERED AT REST. The window was between two adjacent commands and nothing read the projection in it, but the honest statement is that it existed rather than that it did not matter."}